<%# Shown when the signed-in user has no usable Google ID token —
    either it expired and refresh failed (revoked refresh_token) or
    they never had a refresh_token (signed in before access_type: offline,
    or Google didn't emit one). Any prompt typed before this state was
    detected is stashed in localStorage by chats_form_controller and
    restored after the OAuth round-trip. %>
<div class="reauth-banner" data-controller="reauth-banner">
  <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill"></i>
  <div class="reauth-banner-message">
    Your Google sign-in has expired. Re-sign-in to continue using API-key
    providers (Ollama still works without sign-in). Google will ask you to
    confirm access — granting it lets us renew the sign-in automatically
    instead of asking again every hour.
  </div>
  <%# prompt=consent, not the initializer's select_account.
      Google issues a refresh_token only on the FIRST consent per user x
      client, so a plain re-sign-in returns a fresh id_token and still no
      refresh_token: the banner clears, then the same lapse returns an hour
      later, forever. Forcing the consent screen on this path — and only this
      path, so ordinary sign-in stays frictionless — is what actually restores
      offline access. :prompt is in omniauth-google-oauth2's AUTHORIZE_OPTIONS
      and is read from request params, so this overrides the initializer for
      this request alone. %>
  <%= form_with url: user_google_oauth2_omniauth_authorize_path,
                method: :post, local: true,
                data: { turbo: false, action: "submit->reauth-banner#stashAndSubmit" } do |form| %>
    <%= hidden_field_tag :prompt, "consent" %>
    <%= button_tag type: "submit", class: "reauth-banner-button" do %>
      <i class="fa-brands fa-google"></i> Sign in again
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
